Compiler-owned static traits prove source evidence for explicit casts. The
compiler also registers builtin evidence for its supported source-target policies.

### Source-authored target families

Moth defines an infallible and a fallible evidence trait for each of fifteen
targets: the eleven fixed numeric types, `Bool`, `String`, `Char` and `Error`.
Each infallible trait requires a method returning its target. Each fallible
trait requires a `try_to_*` method returning the target and builtin `Error!`.

The @../traits/#core-cast-traits (Core cast traits) reference owns the complete
fixed-target inventory and method signatures. A source implementation uses an
ordinary lowercase `this` receiver with its concrete type.

### Builtin evidence

Builtin evidence follows the complete conversion policies, including the fixed
numeric targets, `Byte` and the `Dec` family. A known value, folded result,
proven range or optimiser fact never changes the evidence class. A safe value
cannot make a fallible source-target pair infallible.

`Byte` and `Dec` retain their builtin conversion policies but gain no
source-authored evidence families.

### Source-authored evidence

A same-file nominal struct, choice or aligned generic nominal constructor can
provide evidence for one supported target. It must define the required
receiver method and declare explicit conformance:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    Label = |
        text String,
    |

    to_string |this Label| -> String:
        return this.text
    ;

    Label must CASTABLE_TO_STRING

    label = Label("hello")
    text String = cast label
]

Fallible evidence declares its actual typed route:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    try_to_i32 |this Label| -> I32, Error!:
        value I32 = cast this.text
        return value
    ;

    Label must TRY_CASTABLE_TO_I32

    parsed I32 = cast Label("42") catch then 0
]

The cast uses the ordinary failure rules. Builtin conversion failures enter
implicit failure. A fallible source method exposes its declared `Error!`
outcome. An eligible catch or compatible function boundary handles the actual
failure; the cast syntax does not select a separate propagation route.

### Contract

- Core cast traits resolve without dependency clauses.
- They cannot be redeclared, imported, exported, aliased or shadowed.
- Traits are static contracts, not value types.
- Matching method shape alone is not evidence. Explicit conformance is required.
- Same-file nominal types and aligned generic nominal instances may provide source evidence.
- Infallible and fallible evidence for the same source type and target are mutually exclusive.
- Evidence resolves before HIR and follows the complete static source and target types.
- Infallible evidence implementations cannot acquire an inferred failure contract.
- The traits prove source evidence only. They add no user-defined target types or general conversion framework.

### Outside cast design scope

- user-defined target types
- generic cast target parameters
- external opaque cast targets
- generic cast traits
- broad return-type-directed conversion
- conversion search through an intermediate target
- implicit conversion selected from distant context

See @./#cast-targets (Cast targets) for builtin conversion exactness and
@../traits/#trait-incompatibility (Trait incompatibility) for conflicting
trait implementations.
